## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

## Why This Is Asked

This question adds counting of optimal partitions to a standard min-max DP, testing both correctness and edge-case handling in a realistic workload-balancing scenario.

## Key Concepts

- DP with a value-cost pair (minCost, count) and prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation with identical transitions
- Edge-case handling: n<k invalid, zeros, large sums

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartitions(nums, k){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array.from({length:k+1},()=>null));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = {best: pref[i], count:1};
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=2;j<=Math.min(i,k);j++){
      let best = Infinity, cnt = 0;
      for(let t=j-1;t<=i-1;t++){
        const last = pref[i]-pref[t];
        const prev = dp[t][j-1];
        if(!prev) continue;
        const cand = Math.max(prev.best, last);
        if(cand < best){ best = cand; cnt = prev.count; }
        else if(cand === best){ cnt += prev.count; }
      }
      dp[i][j] = {best, count: cnt};
    }
  }
  const res = dp[n][k];
  return res ? {cost: res.best, ways: res.count} : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify to return all partition indices achieving the min cost?
- Could you optimize to O(nk) time with a narrowed search if nums are non-decreasing?

## Why This Is Asked
In practice, data often contains many duplicates. A 3-way partitioned quicksort minimizes comparisons on duplicates and scales with the number of distinct keys.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- Randomized pivot to avoid worst-case
- Complexity: O(N log D) expected when D distinct keys
- In-place sort with minimal extra space

## Code Example
```javascript
function sort3(a, l=0, r=a.length-1){
  if (l >= r) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random()*(r-l+1))+l];
  let i=l, lt=l, gt=r;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  sort3(a, l, lt-1);
  sort3(a, gt+1, r);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for stable sorting with duplicates?
- What are the trade-offs vs a counting/radix approach on integers?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses augmenting a Trie to answer prefix-based queries with dynamic weights while maintaining efficient updates. Encourages careful handling of edge cases and update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with maxSubtreeWeight; non-decreasing weight updates.
- O(len(word)) insert and O(len(s)) query.
- Edge cases: empty word input, overlapping prefixes, no matches.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.wordWeight = 0;
    this.hasWord = false;
    this.maxSubtreeWeight = 0;
  }
}
class PrefixWeightTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new Node(); }
  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new Node());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    if (!node.hasWord || weight > node.wordWeight) {
      node.hasWord = true;
      node.wordWeight = weight;
    }
    // update maxSubtreeWeight along path (omitted detailed propagation for brevity)
  }
  queryLongestPrefix(s) {
    let node = this.root;
    let bestLen = 0, bestWeight = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      const c = s[i];
      if (!node.next.has(c)) break;
      node = node.next.get(c);
      if (node.maxSubtreeWeight > 0) {
        bestLen = i + 1;
        bestWeight = node.maxSubtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return [bestLen, bestWeight];
  }
}
```

